          "settings": {
            "text": "Why parents still look for a cassette player",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "heading_tag_html": "h2",
            "text_color": "",
            "gradient": ""
          }
        },
        "why_text": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>A children's cassette player is simple. The child is in control: they choose a story, put it in and press play. There is no screen and no phone, you own the story, and it all works offline. That is why parents still look for one.</p><p>The trouble is everything else: tapes that stretch and tangle, fewer new children's stories on tape, second-hand players that wear out, and batteries that run flat.</p><p>Voxblock keeps all four. It doesn't play cassettes. Each story is a solid cardboard audiobook that sits on the player, so there is no tape to tangle. It is a library you can hold and keep on the shelf, and it goes in the car or on the plane.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
